    answer: "Appwrite stores only a salted Argon2 hash of the password, the same way it stores account passwords, and the API never returns it. Changing the password ends every existing clearance for that rule immediately."
---

Not everything you deploy is ready for the public. A redesign that launches next week, a preview you share with a client, an admin dashboard, or a function that only your cron job should call. They all need the same thing: keep strangers out, let the right people in, and do it without building sign-in into an app that does not need it.

Until now, that meant adding an auth layer to the app, hiding it behind an unguessable URL, or putting another proxy in front of it.

Today, we are announcing the **Firewall password challenge**. Add a password to any [site](/docs/products/sites) or [function](/docs/products/functions) from the Firewall page, and Appwrite asks for it before the request reaches your code.

# What shipped

Firewall [challenge](/docs/products/firewall/actions#challenge) rules now have a **challenge type**. The existing **Compute** type stays as it was: the browser solves a proof-of-work puzzle to filter out bots. The new **Password** type asks for a password you choose.

A password rule is an ordinary Firewall rule, so everything you already know applies:

- **Conditions** decide what it covers. Leave them empty to protect a whole site or function, or match a path such as `/admin` to protect one area.
- **Schedules** decide when it applies, for example only outside office hours.
- **Priority** decides how it stacks with your other rules.

# What visitors see

A browser that opens a protected page gets a password page. The right password sets a clearance cookie for that rule, and the visitor is not asked again until the rule's **TTL** runs out (at least 15 minutes, up to 24 hours).

A clearance opens only the rule that issued it. Passing the password for `/preview` does not open `/admin`, even on the same site.

When you change the password, every existing clearance for that rule ends immediately. Anyone who had the old password has to enter the new one on their next request.

# Scripts, servers, and CI send a header

Cron jobs, deploy checks, and backend services cannot fill in a form. They send the password in the `X-Appwrite-WAF-Password` header instead:

```sh
SITE_URL="https://example.com"
PASSWORD="your-password"
curl "$SITE_URL/admin/report" \
  -H "X-Appwrite-WAF-Password: $PASSWORD"
```

A correct password passes and also sets the clearance cookie. A wrong one gets a `403` with no page. Appwrite removes the header before the request reaches your site or function, so your code never sees the password. The header is checked only against the rule that matched the request.

In the browser, use the password page. A cross-origin `fetch` call that sets this header fails the CORS preflight.

# Set it up in the Console

1. Open **Firewall** in your project.
2. Click **Create rule** and pick the site or function to protect.
3. Add conditions if you only want to protect part of it.
4. Under **Then**, choose **Challenge** and set **Challenge type** to **Password**.
5. Enter a password of 8 to 256 bytes. Some characters, like emoji or non-Latin letters, take more than one byte.
6. Set **Wrong guesses allowed**, **Counted over (seconds)**, and **TTL (seconds)**, or keep the defaults.
7. Click **Create rule**.

The rules list shows the challenge type and its guess limit, for example `Password · 10 guesses / 5 min`. To change the password later, update the rule and enter a new one. Leave the field blank to keep the current password.

You can also create password rules through the API with `POST /v1/waf/rules/challenge` and `challengeType` set to `password`. The [docs](/docs/products/firewall/password-protect#create-the-rule-through-the-api) have the full request.

# Guess limits

Each rule limits wrong guesses. By default, a visitor network gets **10** wrong guesses every **5 minutes**. You can set anywhere from 1 to 100 guesses over 60 seconds to 24 hours.

Guesses are counted per IP address for IPv4 and per `/64` for IPv6, so rotating through the addresses of one IPv6 network does not buy more tries. Guesses through the page and the header share the same count. Over the limit, Appwrite answers `429` with a `Retry-After` header.

The password itself is stored only as a salted Argon2 hash, the same way Appwrite stores account passwords. The API never returns it.

# When to use it

- **Pre-launch and staging sites.** Share the real deployment with your team before launch day.
- **Client previews.** Send a link and a password instead of creating accounts.
- **Internal tools and admin routes.** Put a password in front of `/admin` without touching the app.
- **Internal function endpoints.** Let cron jobs and CI call a function with a header, and turn away everyone else.
- **Quick access control.** Keep a project private while you decide whether it needs real authentication.

# What it does not do

The password challenge protects the public traffic of sites and functions. It does not protect Appwrite API endpoints: the API refuses password rules on the API resource. For example, `POST /v1/functions/{functionId}/executions` follows the function's execute permissions, not the password rule on its domain. For files, use [permissions](/docs/products/storage/permissions) or [file tokens](/docs/products/storage/file-tokens).

A shared password is also not per-user authentication. Everyone with the password gets the same access, and removing one person means changing the password for everyone. When you need individual accounts, use [Appwrite Auth](/docs/products/auth).

# Available today

The password challenge is live on Appwrite Cloud for every project with Firewall. Password rules count toward your plan's [rule limit](/docs/products/firewall/rules#plan-limits) like any other rule.

